[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause found.** There is a rogue iptables FORWARD DROP rule on `router_core_3` blocking all traffic destined for `10.200.0.0/24`. The counter shows 12 packets (1 008 bytes = 12 × 84 B ICMP echoes — our own pings) already silently dropped. That is why `router_core_3` itself can ping the DNS server (its own pings use the OUTPUT/INPUT chains, not FORWARD), but any host traffic forwarded _through_ it is black-holed.

```
Chain FORWARD (policy ACCEPT)
num   pkts bytes target  prot opt in  out  source    destination
1       12  1008 DROP    0    --  *   *    0.0.0.0/0  10.200.0.0/24
```

This is consistent with every symptom: both pods affected (all host traffic transits router_core_3 to reach the server farm), pod-to-pod works (those paths don't hit this rule), OSPF is healthy (control plane, not data plane), and the outage started during a firewall change window.

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_3
diagnosis_text:
A spurious iptables FORWARD chain rule was inserted on router_core_3 during the morning firewall change window. The rule is a blanket DROP matching all protocols from any source to the entire 10.200.0.0/24 server-farm subnet (`iptables -A FORWARD -d 10.200.0.0/24 -j DROP`). Every packet that a host sends toward the server farm arrives at router_core_3 and is forwarded by the kernel toward eth2 (the link to switch_server_access); before it leaves, netfilter evaluates the FORWARD chain and drops it silently. The 12-packet / 1 008-byte counter on that rule — accumulated from our own diagnostic pings — confirms it is actively exercised. Because the rule lives in the FORWARD chain, traffic *originated by router_core_3 itself* (e.g. our test ping from that device) passes through the OUTPUT chain and is unaffected, which is why router_core_3 can reach dns_server but hosts cannot. Pod-to-pod reachability is unaffected because inter-pod traffic is routed through router_core_1/router_core_2 and never hits this rule. OSPF and the routing table remain correct throughout — this is a pure data-plane firewall fault. The corrective fix is to flush the offending rule on router_core_3: `iptables -D FORWARD -d 10.200.0.0/24 -j DROP`. Persistence should be verified (e.g. in /etc/iptables/rules.v4 or the startup script) to prevent the rule from reloading on reboot.
=== END ===